Cantor pairing is inverted by locating the least triangle bucket that contains a code and reading off the residual. The formalization uses a search lemma and two equivalent presentations of that search, then defines the computational unpair map.

**Definition (`triangle`, from `pair.lean`).** $\mathrm{triangle}(w)=w(w+1)/2$. The codes with diagonal sum $w$ occupy the half-open interval $[\mathrm{triangle}(w),\,\mathrm{triangle}(w+1))$.

**Definition (`unpairWRec`).** Recursion on $n$: $\mathrm{unpairWRec}(k,0)=0$, and
$$\mathrm{unpairWRec}(k,n+1)=\begin{cases}\mathrm{unpairWRec}(k,n)&\text{if }k<\mathrm{triangle}(n+1),\\ n+1&\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}$$
This advances the candidate bucket until $k$ lies below $\mathrm{triangle}(w+1)$.

**Definition (`unpairW`).** $\mathrm{unpairW}(k)=\mathrm{unpairWRec}(k,k)$.

**Definition (`unpairComp`).** With $w=\mathrm{Nat.find}(\mathrm{exists\_triangle\_succ}\,k)$ and $m=k-\mathrm{triangle}(w)$, set $\mathrm{unpairComp}(k)=(w-m,\,m)$.

**Theorem (`exists_triangle_succ`).** For every $k\in\omega$ there is a $w$ with $k < \mathrm{triangle}(w+1)$.

*Proof.* Take $w=k$. Then $\mathrm{triangle}(k+1)=\mathrm{triangle}(k)+(k+1)$, so $k < k+1 \le \mathrm{triangle}(k+1)$. ∎

**Theorem (`unpairWRec.succ`).** Unfolding the successor step:
$$\mathrm{unpairWRec}(k,n+1)=\begin{cases}\mathrm{unpairWRec}(k,n)&\text{if }k<\mathrm{triangle}(n+1),\\ n+1&\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}$$

*Proof.* By definition of `Nat.rec`. ∎

**Theorem (`unpairWRec.lt`).** For all $k,n$, either $k < \mathrm{triangle}(\mathrm{unpairWRec}(k,n)+1)$ or $\mathrm{unpairWRec}(k,n)=n$.

*Proof.* Induction on $n$. At $0$, case-split on $k<\mathrm{triangle}(1)$. At $n+1$, if $k<\mathrm{triangle}(n+1)$ then the recursive value inherits the claim from the inductive hypothesis (and if that value equals $n$, the same inequality $k<\mathrm{triangle}(n+1)$ applies); otherwise the value is $n+1$. ∎

**Theorem (`unpairWRec.min`).** If $m < \mathrm{unpairWRec}(k,n)$, then $\neg(k < \mathrm{triangle}(m+1))$.

*Proof.* Induction on $n$, generalizing $m$. At $0$ the hypothesis $m<\mathrm{unpairWRec}(k,0)$ is impossible. At $n+1$, if $k<\mathrm{triangle}(n+1)$ reduce to the inductive hypothesis; otherwise $\mathrm{unpairWRec}(k,n+1)=n+1$, so $m\le n$, and any $k<\mathrm{triangle}(m+1)$ would force $k<\mathrm{triangle}(n+1)$ by monotonicity of $\mathrm{triangle}$, a contradiction. ∎

**Theorem (`unpairW.eq`).** $\mathrm{unpairW}(k)=\mathrm{Nat.find}(\mathrm{exists\_triangle\_succ}\,k)$.

*Proof.* Show $\mathrm{unpairWRec}(k,k)$ satisfies the characterizing properties of $\mathrm{Nat.find}$: it is a witness (`unpairWRec.lt`, using $k+1\le\mathrm{triangle}(k+1)$ when the residual case $\mathrm{unpairWRec}(k,k)=k$ occurs), and no smaller index works (`unpairWRec.min`). ∎

**Theorem (`unpairComp.eq`).** $\mathrm{unpairComp}(k)=\mathrm{unpair}(k)$.

*Proof.* Let $w$ be that find-witness and $m=k-\mathrm{triangle}(w)$. Minimality of $w$ gives $\mathrm{triangle}(w)\le k$, and $k<\mathrm{triangle}(w+1)$ gives $m\le w$. Then $(w-m)+m=w$, so $\mathrm{pair}(w-m,\,m)=k$ by the triangle form of pairing. Apply `unpair_pair`. ∎
